1
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:03,360
That would be great.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:05,920
Perfect.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:10,320
So welcome everybody to this month's seminar.

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:18,240
This month we have Dr. Collins Mindendo presenting, and he's going to be speaking about security and privacy in the underserved majority world.

5
00:00:18,446 --> 00:00:22,756
So practices, challenges, and opportunities from his research.

6
00:00:24,174 --> 00:00:32,640
So recent developments in security and privacy have emphasized the need to consider human factors in security and privacy research and design.

7
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:45,600
At the same time, however, most of the existing body of work is focused on Western populations, particularly in the US and Europe, despite other research showing that security and privacy perceptions and practices vary across countries and cultures.

8
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:55,044
So consequently, the security and privacy solutions designed and evaluated in the West can quickly fall apart when they're used or applied in other regions in the majority world.

9
00:00:55,360 --> 00:01:02,000
So in his talk, Collins will draw from a series of studies across the majority world to highlight unique security and privacy challenges.

10
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:08,644
The first part will focus on Kenya, examining privacy tensions with mobile loan applications and cyber cafes.

11
00:01:08,846 --> 00:01:15,300
And the second part will go beyond Kenya to show how these challenges recur across majority world contexts.

12
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:23,760
Finally, Collins will discuss solutions to these challenges before sharing promising directions for his future work and our future work as well.

13
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:27,940
So just a bit about Collins and then I'll hand over to him.

14
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:38,160
He's a postdoctoral scholar at the University of California, Berkeley, and he's an incoming professor of computer science at the National University of Singapore.

15
00:01:38,766 --> 00:01:46,720
His research focuses on human-centered security and privacy, and he publishes a flagship conferences in computer security.

16
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:48,320
I don't know why my slides keep doing that.

17
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:51,200
Privacy and human computer interaction.

18
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:55,280
He earned his PhD in computer science from George Washington University.

19
00:01:55,534 --> 00:01:58,244
So I will hand it over to you now, Collins.

20
00:01:59,360 --> 00:01:59,680
Yeah.

21
00:02:00,014 --> 00:02:05,280
Thank you so much, Stephanie, for that very kind introduction.

22
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,500
So let me just pull up my screen.

23
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:17,120
Can you see my screen.

24
00:02:21,198 --> 00:02:21,560
Yes,

25
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:22,080
we can.

26
00:02:22,286 --> 00:02:28,080
And I'll ask all participants to just mute themselves to allow for Collins to present.

27
00:02:30,798 --> 00:02:31,280
Awesome.

28
00:02:31,566 --> 00:02:35,680
So yeah, good morning, good afternoon, good evening, everyone.

29
00:02:35,930 --> 00:02:43,396
I'm very excited to be here today to speak about some of the research that I've been doing over the last six years.

30
00:02:45,614 --> 00:02:50,720
So humans are very central and very essential to security and privacy.

31
00:02:50,926 --> 00:02:55,172
We are often the ones designing and building security and privacy solutions.

32
00:02:55,502 --> 00:02:59,600
We are also the same people that actually use other tools.

33
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:07,684
And so it's actually very important to study and understand the role that humans play in this whole security and privacy ecosystem.

34
00:03:08,878 --> 00:03:30,532
And unsurprisingly, there's been a recent number of studies, particularly focusing on human factors and really trying to understand how humans interact with, use, and even misuse security and privacy tools, ultimately, with the goal of trying to design solutions that work for us in practice.

35
00:03:31,918 --> 00:03:36,160
But at the same time, a majority of this work has just concentrated in a few places.

36
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:42,640
And so this chart that you see here is from a paper that was published at Usenic Security two years ago.

37
00:03:42,862 --> 00:03:49,348
And it basically shows where human-centered security and privacy studies have recruited participants from.

38
00:03:49,582 --> 00:03:52,480
And this is also normalized to the population.

39
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:58,800
And you quickly see that a majority of this work has mostly concentrated in Western populations.

40
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:11,524
And so the parts that I want you to focus on are the ones highlighted in orange, and you can see that the US and Canada have accounted for a majority of this work and some parts of Western Europe as well.

41
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:21,552
And a side effect of this is that most security and privacy design and choices actually make Western assumptions.

42
00:04:21,712 --> 00:04:25,828
And sometimes these assumptions don't always hold in the rest of the world.

43
00:04:26,352 --> 00:04:37,188
And if everyone is actually using this solution, sometimes they don't necessarily serve them the way they were expected or designed to in the first place.

44
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:48,932
And so a big part of my work has tried to go beyond Western horizons to also explore how security and privacy looks like in the rest of the world.

45
00:04:49,872 --> 00:04:54,432
And in particular, I'm going to use the rest of the world.

46
00:04:54,752 --> 00:05:02,980
I'm going to refer to the rest of the world as the majority world in this context, because they do, in fact, account for a majority of the world's population.

47
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:15,312
And in doing my research, I actually leverage a very interdisciplinary set of methods drawn from human-computer interaction, but also from traditional computer security and privacy.

48
00:05:15,502 --> 00:05:25,348
And this includes more qualitative methods such as interviews and focus groups, but I also use quantitative methods, for example, surveys and experiments.

49
00:05:25,632 --> 00:05:30,032
And ultimately, the goal of my work is to actually design and build better solutions.

50
00:05:30,254 --> 00:05:35,876
And so here I'm often using methods including prototyping but also software development.

51
00:05:37,198 --> 00:05:42,752
And my recent body of work has focused on a number of different directions, ranging from authentication, right?

52
00:05:42,912 --> 00:05:50,628
How do users select pins and passwords, all the way again to underserved users, for example, in the majority world?

53
00:05:50,992 --> 00:05:56,036
And in particular, this is what I'm going to be focusing on for this talk today.

54
00:05:57,632 --> 00:06:02,192
And so just to quickly highlight other things I'm going to talk about today.

55
00:06:02,446 --> 00:06:06,032
So my talk today will start with a case study of Kenya.

56
00:06:06,158 --> 00:06:15,952
And I'm going to particularly highlight two studies that are going to show you how socioeconomic challenges can influence people's privacy concerns and perceptions.

57
00:06:16,238 --> 00:06:27,712
And in particular, we're going to see how financial constraints and challenges can actually make people deprioritize their privacy concerns when it comes to using a loan application.

58
00:06:28,046 --> 00:06:38,436
And then we also see how cyber cafes, even though they're like very helpful, can also introduce privacy tensions and people have to share computing facilities.

59
00:06:39,502 --> 00:06:54,276
And then from there, I'm going to zoom out a bit and see whether these challenges are just unique to Kenya or whether they also exist in other majority contexts and what that means when it comes to designing those solutions.

60
00:06:54,734 --> 00:07:06,512
And then finally, I'm going to try and end on a positive note where I'll share some of the work that I've been doing in trying to redesign some existing technologies to be more privacy preserving.

61
00:07:06,592 --> 00:07:15,844
And I'll also share some future work where I'll show you how we can use privacy-enhancing technologies to allow data sharing in the majority world.

62
00:07:17,262 --> 00:07:20,644
Okay, so let's start by talking about mobile loan applications.

63
00:07:21,166 --> 00:07:35,748
So just to give you a quick background about these apps, these are essentially apps that you can download from either the Google Play Store but also the Apple Play Store and sometimes even third-party markets with the goal of procuring a loan.

64
00:07:36,032 --> 00:07:47,712
And these apps have become very common in majority of countries, for example, Kenya, partly because people don't often have a formal credit history.

65
00:07:47,872 --> 00:08:06,432
And so this the gap that these apps fill is that they'll often just require you to download an app, they'll request access to some data points on your phone, they'll compute a credit worth, they'll compute credit worthiness for you, and within a few minutes or seconds, they can offer you a loan.

66
00:08:06,606 --> 00:08:17,348
And this app has also been partly enabled by the success of mobile money services like MPES and Kenya, which allows them to quickly disperse uh small loans to users.

67
00:08:18,894 --> 00:08:29,792
But at the same time, there's been numerous reports showing just very strange ways that these apps try to get people to repay their loans.

68
00:08:30,032 --> 00:08:38,083
and they often use tactics including, you know, shaming users and so on, which is obviously very concerning.

69
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:41,392
And this is what motivated us to do this study.

70
00:08:41,645 --> 00:08:48,388
So, in particular, we just wanted to get a sense of what some of the most common loan apps uh in Kenya actually are.

71
00:08:48,912 --> 00:09:02,276
and then the second part of this study was more of a user study where we wanted to understand why people choose to use these apps and some of the privacy concerns and trade offs they have they have to make in doing so.

72
00:09:03,694 --> 00:09:08,960
And so at the time of doing this study, we found that loan apps were in fact very common.

73
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:22,080
And at the time of doing this, maybe three or four years ago, we found that Tala and Branch were in fact the most commonly used apps, both by participants in our study, but just generally as well.

74
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:27,840
And at the time, Tala had more than 5 million downloads, and Branch had more than 10 million downloads.

75
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,788
And this is just on the Google Play Store alone.

76
00:09:31,150 --> 00:09:38,180
And at the same time, these apps were also used by at least half of the participants that we talked to as part of the study.

77
00:09:40,014 --> 00:09:48,260
But at the same time, we also observed that these apps constantly required access to very sensitive permissions from users.

78
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:57,412
And for all the eight common apps that we reviewed, we found that they all needed access to users' contacts, SMS, location, and storage.

79
00:09:58,478 --> 00:10:05,680
But what was maybe concerning was the fact that these apps did not seem to transparently disclose to users how they use this information.

80
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:19,520
And so when we reviewed their privacy policies, for example, and we also talked to people, it became apparent that these apps were not honestly and transparently informing users how they were going to use this information.

81
00:10:20,942 --> 00:10:24,644
And just to again maybe highlight some sentiments in the user studies.

82
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:31,760
So we found that many people actually had a lot of privacy concerns when it came to using these applications.

83
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:38,596
And in particular, many users seemed to be very much concerned about this app's access to their contacts.

84
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:51,520
And as P1 highlighted here, when you default in repayment, this app will often start calling your brothers and sisters and other people in your contact list just because they require access to that information.

85
00:10:51,680 --> 00:11:00,196
But as I said earlier, they often won't tell you that this is in fact how your information is going to be used, which is obviously concerning.

86
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:14,948
And this was also echoed by P11, who basically told us that this apps essentially went after their father-in-law, mostly to try and make her embarrassed so that she could repay the loan.

87
00:11:16,654 --> 00:11:27,780
But what was even more interesting is that participants seemed to be happy to suppress or deprioritize their privacy concerns just because they were motivated to get the loans.

88
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:36,740
And as highlighted by P1 here again, they told us that when you really need the money, you won't care about privacy issues.

89
00:11:37,774 --> 00:11:43,520
And this was also echoed by P11, who told us that desperate times really call for desperate measures.

90
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:52,800
And so, again, when you really need the money, sometimes you have to put your privacy concerns to one side and just do what the apps need you to do, right?

91
00:11:52,880 --> 00:12:02,116
So you don't even know what they want to do with your data, but you really need the money in this case, and so you just have to go ahead and give them access to what they want.

92
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:07,460
So, what are some of the things uh or some of the takeaways we took from this study?

93
00:12:07,822 --> 00:12:13,920
So, first of all, we are not by any means the first set of researchers to look at mobile applications.

94
00:12:14,638 --> 00:12:21,120
There's been, in fact, a whole lot of studies trying to explore mobile applications.

95
00:12:21,454 --> 00:12:34,160
And what's really interesting is that a lot of these studies, especially the ones that have been conducted in the West, they often advocate for giving users more control and more transparency on how the information is being used.

96
00:12:34,478 --> 00:12:50,788
But in this study, we actually argue that just giving users more transparency and more control is not necessarily going to protect their privacy because, as we saw earlier, people are actually willing to give away this privacy because they want to procure the loan.

97
00:12:50,990 --> 00:12:57,730
And so I believe that platforms, for example, uh Google, but also regulators can actually step in and protect users, Like?

98
00:12:57,840 --> 00:13:11,364
Whether this is, you know, preventing access to very sensitive information or just making sure that these apps do, in fact, more clearly detail how this information will be used.

99
00:13:12,014 --> 00:13:18,372
And after I published this study, I had an opportunity to speak with some reporters from TechCrunch.

100
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:27,920
And I also had some informal conversations with just some privacy engineers at Google when I went to present this work at SP.

101
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:33,840
And more recently, we've actually started to see some platforms take some action.

102
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:40,400
And so, for example, right now, Google actually prevents these apps from accessing users' contacts, right?

103
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:49,600
When you download one of these apps today, I believe they cannot access your contacts because this is information that they've routinely been misusing.

104
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:54,960
And I think this is a promising first step in protecting privacy for users in the space.

105
00:13:55,040 --> 00:14:00,420
Obviously, there's still more work to be done, but this is a promising first step.

106
00:14:02,318 --> 00:14:07,780
So we've seen how limited resources can make people deprioritize their privacy concerns.

107
00:14:08,526 --> 00:14:14,144
But these limited resources also extend way beyond just applications that people use.

108
00:14:14,304 --> 00:14:19,044
This can also extend to even how people access technology in the first place.

109
00:14:19,950 --> 00:14:28,484
And so now I'm going to talk about some of the challenges that can be introduced, and people have to share computing resources, for example, at CyberCafes.

110
00:14:29,198 --> 00:14:42,340
So for those of you who might not know what cyber cafes are, these are facilities that are usually equipped with computers and printers, where customers can pay to access the internet, but also perform other computer-related tasks.

111
00:14:43,024 --> 00:14:51,424
And these facilities have become extremely popular in Kenya because of recent digitization of government services.

112
00:14:51,664 --> 00:14:57,220
That's also coupled with low ownership of personal computers or personal devices in general.

113
00:14:57,806 --> 00:15:07,940
And even for folks that have their own smartphones, because a lot of the services usually require you to print your documents after you've filled out the long government forms.

114
00:15:08,304 --> 00:15:14,944
A lot of folks will still go to these facilities when they have to print those forms as well.

115
00:15:15,566 --> 00:15:25,424
But at the same time, the shared nature of these facilities often introduces a lot of challenges when it comes to authentication, but also privacy in general.

116
00:15:25,504 --> 00:15:28,740
And this is what we wanted to explore as part of this study.

117
00:15:29,744 --> 00:15:32,224
And so just a quick overview of how we did this.

118
00:15:32,384 --> 00:15:38,784
So we conducted semi-structured interviews with both managers and customers of these facilities.

119
00:15:39,086 --> 00:15:43,664
So managers are just the folks that usually manage these facilities.

120
00:15:43,824 --> 00:15:49,860
Well, on the other hand, customers are the folks that go to seek services from these places.

121
00:15:50,144 --> 00:15:57,584
And I essentially spent a summer in Kenya going from one cyber cafe to another, trying to talk to these folks.

122
00:15:57,744 --> 00:16:06,864
Again, just to understand from their perspective what were some of the challenges, but also to try and observe what was actually going on in these facilities, right?

123
00:16:07,104 --> 00:16:13,828
Do they update the operating systems, for example, what are some of the authentication measures they have?

124
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:19,940
Okay, so let's jump into some results here, starting with some customer challenges.

125
00:16:20,302 --> 00:16:26,276
So one of the most commonly mentioned challenge at these facilities is around technology usage.

126
00:16:26,734 --> 00:16:33,360
And as you can see from this quote here from Manager 10, a lot of customers will go to this facility.

127
00:16:33,504 --> 00:16:39,744
Some of them may have never used a computer before and they want to do a given task.

128
00:16:39,824 --> 00:16:43,652
And this is usually very challenging for them.

129
00:16:43,886 --> 00:16:46,784
And in fact, it usually leads to other issues as well.

130
00:16:46,944 --> 00:16:54,304
And in particular, account creation usually becomes a big challenge for a lot of this uh customers, right?

131
00:16:54,414 --> 00:17:05,424
So a lot of websites are usually often designed with very specific password requirements where users have to have a mix of uppercase characters and all those things.

132
00:17:05,806 --> 00:17:10,064
But sometimes this can be very challenging for someone who's using a computer for the first place.

133
00:17:10,223 --> 00:17:17,664
I think many of us take it for granted that we can, for example, press the shift key and type an uppercase character.

134
00:17:17,743 --> 00:17:22,948
But for someone who's actually never used a computer, this is in fact very challenging.

135
00:17:23,278 --> 00:17:30,624
And because of issues with account creation, this usually leads to issues with account access as well, right?

136
00:17:30,894 --> 00:17:39,744
So again, from this manager here, they told us that people will even forget the emails or the phone numbers that they actually use to create the accounts.

137
00:17:40,144 --> 00:17:55,904
And this is really interesting because a lot of the systems have been, or a lot of account recovery systems often assume that people are at least going to remember the email address that was used to create the account so they can send the reset instructions.

138
00:17:56,064 --> 00:18:06,628
But in this case, we actually found that some customers even forget what email was used to set up the account, and this can often lead to issues trying to recover access to these accounts.

139
00:18:08,110 --> 00:18:17,380
And when trying to tackle these challenges, a lot of customers often rely on the support and advice provided by cyber cafe managers.

140
00:18:17,904 --> 00:18:23,904
And in particular, password selection is something that a lot of managers help their customers with.

141
00:18:23,984 --> 00:18:30,464
So when we think about passwords, we often assume that people are going to select passwords by themselves, right?

142
00:18:30,544 --> 00:18:35,684
So passwords are supposed to be secret, and only you should know what password you've selected.

143
00:18:36,078 --> 00:18:42,756
We also often assume or imagine that people are going to select very complex passwords so that they cannot be easily guessed.

144
00:18:43,184 --> 00:18:46,372
But we in fact saw the opposite at those facilities.

145
00:18:47,790 --> 00:18:53,488
So, first of all, managers do play an active role in selecting passwords for their customers.

146
00:18:53,568 --> 00:19:10,692
As you can see from this manager here, they actually select passwords for a majority of their customers, which is really interesting because now this password is known not just to the customer, but also to this manager that assisted them to create it in the first place.

147
00:19:12,128 --> 00:19:25,808
And because managers are often assisting several customers, they end up in this situation where they have to select a password that is memorable not just to the customer, but also to the manager as well.

148
00:19:25,968 --> 00:19:35,408
And, you know, what a lot of managers, in fact, end up doing is to try and find or try to select a password that has something that is going to be memorable to the user.

149
00:19:35,662 --> 00:19:43,248
And it turns out that ID numbers are actually a very common password that managers recommend to customers.

150
00:19:43,470 --> 00:19:53,328
Again, which is really interesting because, you know, at least in the Kenyan context, when you visit a building, for example, you often need to provide your ID number when you're checking in.

151
00:19:53,408 --> 00:20:00,128
When you transact with mobile money, agents often need access to your IDs and they often record this number down.

152
00:20:00,208 --> 00:20:02,928
And so this is the password that you're using.

153
00:20:03,008 --> 00:20:06,244
This can lead to some issues down the line.

154
00:20:07,310 --> 00:20:13,060
And then lastly, we also saw some potentially harmful advice or practices at cyber cafes.

155
00:20:13,648 --> 00:20:15,888
And the first one is just about saving passwords.

156
00:20:16,048 --> 00:20:26,244
So we talked to a number of customers and some managers as well who told us that they've either been advised or advised their customers to save passwords in the cyber cafe computers.

157
00:20:26,542 --> 00:20:35,140
Again, this is really interesting because, on one hand, whenever they go back to the cyber cafe they don't need to remember what their password was.

158
00:20:35,408 --> 00:20:43,620
But because a lot of these facilities are often public and there's a lot of people streaming in, this can pose a lot of security issues.

159
00:20:45,198 --> 00:20:49,444
And then lastly, we saw a very interesting case of password reuse.

160
00:20:49,968 --> 00:21:00,528
So, you know, I've done a lot of authentication studies in the West, and password reuse is a constant challenge, but it's often one user reusing the same password for multiple accounts.

161
00:21:00,688 --> 00:21:10,500
But what was really interesting in the Cyback affairs is that sometimes you have a manager that essentially reuses the same password for multiple customers.

162
00:21:10,862 --> 00:21:13,888
And again, this is actually supposed to help the manager.

163
00:21:13,968 --> 00:21:20,848
So when a customer just randomly calls and they ask what their password is, they can basically recite that same password to them.

164
00:21:21,008 --> 00:21:30,660
But as you can imagine, this is obviously harmful because the same password being used for multiple people essentially poses security risks.

165
00:21:31,758 --> 00:21:35,648
Okay, so what are the what are some of the takeaways and implications of this work?

166
00:21:35,790 --> 00:21:44,768
So, first of all, we see how this sharing of devices uh at cafes can actually make authentication uh challenging, right?

167
00:21:44,848 --> 00:21:57,568
And so some of the built-in assumptions that we have about authentication, for example, and in particular, you know, we often advise people to use password managers to help them manage their passwords more effectively.

168
00:21:57,728 --> 00:22:06,768
But one assumption we usually make in that process is the fact that that password manager is going to be installed across all the different devices they use.

169
00:22:06,958 --> 00:22:18,916
And so when people have to go to facilities like cyber Cafes where those computers don't have that password manager, it becomes very challenging for that advice to even be useful in the first place.

170
00:22:20,142 --> 00:22:31,536
And then we also kind of see this mismatch, I would say, between what advice is provided in Western contexts versus what local experts in this context actually provide, right?

171
00:22:31,856 --> 00:22:36,736
Again, in the West, you see people being told to use complex passwords and so on.

172
00:22:36,896 --> 00:22:50,096
But local experts, on the other hand, so you can imagine the cyber cafe manager, they'll often try to customize their advice based on the existing constraints, which is a reasonable thing to make, I would argue.

173
00:22:51,662 --> 00:23:06,096
Okay, so we've kind of like seen a few challenges so far with you know people deprioritizing their privacy concerns in the case of mobile learn apps, but also some of these privacy and authentication challenges at cyber cafes.

174
00:23:06,766 --> 00:23:09,536
But are these challenges just limited to Kenya?

175
00:23:09,616 --> 00:23:11,696
Are these unique to Kenya by any means?

176
00:23:11,856 --> 00:23:13,696
Do they also extend to other countries?

177
00:23:13,936 --> 00:23:18,692
This is the next question that we had after doing these first two studies.

178
00:23:19,758 --> 00:23:29,936
So now I'm going to talk about a study that we did where we tried to go beyond Kenya to see if these challenges even exist in other places as well.

179
00:23:30,318 --> 00:23:37,776
And in particular after doing this study around cyber affairs, we came up with this idea of local experts in the majority world.

180
00:23:37,856 --> 00:23:43,776
So you can imagine the CyberCaffer manager, for example, is actually a local expert, right?

181
00:23:43,856 --> 00:23:59,456
So even though this individual might not have any computer science training or any computer security and privacy training for that case, people actually still rely on them, not just for tech support, but also they get security and privacy advice.

182
00:23:59,536 --> 00:24:04,336
And this can have far-reaching uh implications on their digital security and privacy.

183
00:24:04,416 --> 00:24:17,136
And so after we did this study, we started talking uh to some other folks, and it turns out that, you know, it's not just cyber cafe managers where people get advice from.

184
00:24:17,216 --> 00:24:26,016
There's also other individuals, both within Kenya but also across other countries where we are talking to people where these local experts exist.

185
00:24:26,096 --> 00:24:35,456
And this can range from, again, the Cyber Cafe Manager, but also it can include people that sell mobile devices or SIM cards, for example.

186
00:24:35,822 --> 00:24:39,216
It can also extend to people that work in phone repair shift sites.

187
00:24:39,296 --> 00:24:44,016
So, like all these are individuals that people often turn on for advice.

188
00:24:44,096 --> 00:24:50,656
And these are particularly prevalent in the majority world, and maybe more so compared to uh Western context.

189
00:24:50,816 --> 00:24:53,988
And so, as part of this study, there's a couple of questions.

190
00:24:54,256 --> 00:25:07,776
we were interested in, but I really wanted to understand what was some of the advice, or what is some of the advice that local experts actually provide in the majority world, particularly as it relates to security and privacy.

191
00:25:08,014 --> 00:25:12,896
and what implications can that advice have on users' digital security and privacy?

192
00:25:13,262 --> 00:25:22,416
And so, yeah, the first question was what is the advice that these experts actually prioritize when supporting people in their local communities?

193
00:25:22,670 --> 00:25:28,388
And then, secondly, what barriers do people face when trying to implement this advice?

194
00:25:29,806 --> 00:25:36,816
And so, as part of this study, we recruited 70 local experts across nine diverse majority world countries.

195
00:25:36,976 --> 00:25:43,268
And so, you know, we had we talked to local experts in Latin America and in particularly Mexico and Brazil.

196
00:25:43,630 --> 00:25:52,256
We also talked to some local experts uh on the African continent, and that is particularly from Ghana, Nigeria, and Kenya.

197
00:25:52,526 --> 00:25:55,616
We also had some from the Middle East and in particular Turkey.

198
00:25:55,696 --> 00:26:05,828
And then lastly, we had some more experts from Asia, and that was essentially China, India, and Pakistan.

199
00:26:06,574 --> 00:26:12,576
And I also just want to highlight that these nine countries alone account for 50% of the world's population.

200
00:26:12,736 --> 00:26:22,656
Obviously, this is very much inflated by China and India, but it's still interesting that just these nine countries alone make up half of the world's population.

201
00:26:23,662 --> 00:26:30,816
and in trying to do this study, we were very intentional about trying to actually partner and work with local communities.

202
00:26:30,896 --> 00:26:34,532
And so we ended up recruiting local academic researchers.

203
00:26:34,816 --> 00:26:39,376
In some instances, some of them did not have any formal academic training.

204
00:26:39,456 --> 00:26:44,836
And so we had to spend some time just teaching them some of the methods that we actually use.

205
00:26:45,216 --> 00:26:51,056
And the reason for this was to try and make sure some of these benefits extended down to local communities.

206
00:26:51,216 --> 00:27:04,256
I think one common thing that we see in the research community is that researchers from here in the US, for example, will go to Pakistan, do a study, get a shiny cool paper out of it, and that's everything.

207
00:27:04,336 --> 00:27:15,076
And so for this study, we wanted to partner and work with local communities and have them recognized as co authors as well, just so that some of the research benefits would also extend to those communities.

208
00:27:15,952 --> 00:27:18,820
Okay, so let me jump into some of the results.

209
00:27:19,694 --> 00:27:25,152
So we saw a wide variety of advice that local experts prioritize in the majority world.

210
00:27:25,232 --> 00:27:33,072
And when I talk about prioritize, I just mean the advice they have for themselves, but also the advice they give to people when they come to them.

211
00:27:33,312 --> 00:27:45,636
And this advice ranges from again things like passwords all the way to only visiting sites that are known, avoiding public Wi-Fi, changing passwords, and so on and so forth.

212
00:27:46,032 --> 00:27:56,112
And you know, the main takeaway here is that the advice that is actually provided in the majority world actually largely matches security and privacy advice we've kind of like seen in the West.

213
00:27:56,272 --> 00:28:03,940
There's some differences that I'll get to in a minute, but a lot of the advice is actually somewhat similar to what we've seen in the West.

214
00:28:04,718 --> 00:28:11,552
But at the same time, we also saw some advice that is outdated and has potentially been questioned by experts, right?

215
00:28:11,632 --> 00:28:14,272
So things like using an antivirus.

216
00:28:14,432 --> 00:28:21,540
This is a piece of advice that experts don't think is a useful piece of advice anymore.

217
00:28:21,952 --> 00:28:27,632
But at the same time, we also saw advice that is now just considered not good advice anymore.

218
00:28:27,712 --> 00:28:36,512
So regularly changing your passwords, for example, this is advice that is actually burdensome and not necessarily useful for people.

219
00:28:36,592 --> 00:28:46,352
And avoiding public Wi-Fi is also not necessarily a useful piece of advice today, mostly because almost all sites actually use HTTPS.

220
00:28:46,446 --> 00:28:52,132
And so, you know, using public Wi-Fi is not that much of a concern today.

221
00:28:53,454 --> 00:29:00,192
But what was really interesting is that we saw that local experts try to customize their advice.

222
00:29:00,352 --> 00:29:10,244
So when it comes to the advice they give to users, we saw that experts recognize the constraints that exist and try to adapt their advice effectively.

223
00:29:10,512 --> 00:29:29,712
And so, you know, while experts would tell us that they try to use complicated passwords for themselves, they'll often advise people to use memorable passwords just because if you advise someone to select a complex password, for example, and they forget it, they might actually think you gave them bad advice, right?

224
00:29:29,872 --> 00:29:41,668
And so we saw a lot of this customization of advice uh whereby experts recognized some of the constraints and tried to make the advice practical for users.

225
00:29:42,926 --> 00:29:48,032
But at the same time, we also saw a number of challenges when it comes to following uh recommended advice.

226
00:29:48,112 --> 00:29:52,432
And the first one just revolves around socioeconomic challenges, right?

227
00:29:52,592 --> 00:30:01,796
And so one of the pieces of advice that we in the security community like to give to people is to regularly install updates whenever this comes out.

228
00:30:02,222 --> 00:30:08,832
But it actually turns out that this can be challenging to do when people actually have to pay for the internet.

229
00:30:09,072 --> 00:30:19,232
And so, as this local expert in Turkey, for example, told us, most people are just not willing to install updates because they use internet and internet costs money.

230
00:30:19,312 --> 00:30:24,388
And so this is actually a constraint in them trying to implement this piece of advice.

231
00:30:26,062 --> 00:30:31,872
We also saw some social friction that can inhibit people from following our advice.

232
00:30:32,078 --> 00:30:37,952
And this quote here is from a local expert in Pakistan, which I thought was really interesting.

233
00:30:38,272 --> 00:30:43,684
again, we usually advise people to have passwords to protect access to devices or accounts.

234
00:30:43,982 --> 00:30:57,152
But what this expert here told us is that sometimes when you have passwords enabled uh for everything, naturally your family members or your close friends will start questioning if you're hiding anything on your devices.

235
00:30:57,312 --> 00:30:57,472
Right?

236
00:30:57,552 --> 00:31:05,232
So if you're using a lot of passwords, this in itself can actually imply that you're trying to hide something.

237
00:31:05,312 --> 00:31:11,300
And so, you know, this will often discourage people from trying to protect access to everything.

238
00:31:12,592 --> 00:31:20,752
And then lastly, we also saw that language barrier can also inhibit uh people from potentially following advice.

239
00:31:20,912 --> 00:31:26,832
Again, a lot of tech devices and systems, the default language is often uh English.

240
00:31:27,086 --> 00:31:37,472
But what this local expert here told us is that because you know there's going to be errors that are thrown off or alerts that are presented in English, sometimes people don't even know what those mean.

241
00:31:37,552 --> 00:31:44,484
And so the natural instinct or the natural thing for them to do is to try and dismiss those alerts.

242
00:31:45,902 --> 00:31:50,448
Okay, so what are some of the takeaways or implications or things we can learn from the study?

243
00:31:50,670 --> 00:32:02,608
So one of the biggest challenges when it comes to people following advice that other studies have observed in the West is that there's just a wide variety of advice, and sometimes it's hard for people to prioritize.

244
00:32:02,688 --> 00:32:06,048
And this is something that we also observe in the majority world.

245
00:32:06,128 --> 00:32:12,608
There's many different pieces of advice that people mention when we just ask them for their top three pieces of advice.

246
00:32:12,688 --> 00:32:22,628
And we think it's uh we think it's a promising direction to try and figure out which is the most important advice for users to follow in those contexts.

247
00:32:24,128 --> 00:32:31,968
And then secondly, there's just this general need of trying to consider diverse contexts when it comes to designing of security and privacy solutions.

248
00:32:32,048 --> 00:32:42,368
So it's we see how cultural things like you know, social friction and so on can influence even the way people think about protecting access to their devices.

249
00:32:42,448 --> 00:32:50,244
And it's up to our community to try and study some of these factors for us to design solutions that can hopefully work for everyone.

250
00:32:50,848 --> 00:33:00,804
And then lastly, just better translation of not just uh the language used on technology devices, but also advice uh could be useful for users.

251
00:33:02,222 --> 00:33:12,452
So, just to quickly recap again, so we've seen how socioeconomic challenges can make people deprioritize their privacy concerns in the case of uh in the case of mobile loan apps.

252
00:33:13,006 --> 00:33:26,048
We've also seen how socioeconomic challenges can make people rely on shared devices when it comes to uh cyber affairs, but also uh some of the privacy concerns that arise because of that.

253
00:33:26,382 --> 00:33:38,244
And I'm going to argue that in order to tackle some of these challenges, it's actually important that we try and design these tools and these technologies with privacy in mind.

254
00:33:38,528 --> 00:33:42,288
And we actually need to work with local communities in trying to do that.

255
00:33:42,638 --> 00:34:07,140
So now, I'm going to just share some solutions and some future work that I've been thinking about when it comes to building privacy into either existing technologies or trying to find ways in which we can use technologies like privacy-enhancing technologies to allow data sharing and unlock some innovation in the majority world that might not be currently possible.

256
00:34:09,070 --> 00:34:17,888
Okay, so let me now just talk about something that most of you who are in Kenya are going to be very familiar with, which is mobile money services.

257
00:34:17,967 --> 00:34:25,188
And I'm going to show you some of the work that we did where we tried to build privacy into this technology.

258
00:34:26,062 --> 00:34:35,728
So, for those of you maybe who might not be aware of mobile money on the call, this is just a financial system that is often tied to SIM cards or phone numbers, right?

259
00:34:35,888 --> 00:34:49,568
And so you can imagine that when it comes to sending money, the simplest explanation I can possibly give, it's almost like sending a text message to someone, because uh this technology often relies on cell towers.

260
00:34:49,648 --> 00:34:54,368
And so you don't even need a smartphone to actually send money uh when using mobile money.

261
00:34:54,447 --> 00:34:59,888
And this is primarily one of the reasons why it's become very popular in Kenya but also other places.

262
00:35:00,128 --> 00:35:05,408
But at the same time, this technology often involves a lot of third parties known as agents.

263
00:35:05,488 --> 00:35:20,128
So when a lot of people are transacting, you often need to walk up to an agent and then initiate the transaction, and then the agent will either, for example, give you money if you're withdrawing or take your money and help deposit it for you.

264
00:35:20,462 --> 00:35:28,528
But at the same time, this reliance on this third party agents often introduces a lot of privacy risks uh when it comes to transactions.

265
00:35:28,688 --> 00:35:31,728
And let me just walk you through how this looks like.

266
00:35:32,046 --> 00:35:38,436
So it comes to uh transactions, so let's imagine someone wants to withdraw money using mobile money.

267
00:35:38,670 --> 00:35:45,648
So what typically happens is that you have the user on one side and then you have a mobile money agent on the other side.

268
00:35:45,998 --> 00:35:50,448
So in a lot of places, the first thing the user does is to take their ID and hand over.

269
00:35:50,528 --> 00:36:00,848
So like agents will often ask for the user's ID, and this is usually just to authenticate and verify that this is in fact the person they are claiming uh to be.

270
00:36:01,582 --> 00:36:06,128
And then after that, the user will often initiate this transaction from their phone, right?

271
00:36:06,208 --> 00:36:09,008
So let's say they want to withdraw 500 bob.

272
00:36:09,262 --> 00:36:14,372
Uh so they're going to initiate this transaction, put in the agent number, and then uh this.

273
00:36:15,534 --> 00:36:18,916
This transaction will be routed to the mobile money provider.

274
00:36:19,310 --> 00:36:24,128
And then once this transaction is successful, it's going to generate a message.

275
00:36:24,208 --> 00:36:29,408
So one of the messages is going to be sent to the user, but the other one is also going to be sent to the agent.

276
00:36:29,488 --> 00:36:30,980
And it often looks something like this.

277
00:36:31,088 --> 00:36:37,092
So maybe give 500 bob to just a center who's the person, and this is their phone number.

278
00:36:38,190 --> 00:36:48,228
And then after that, what the agent typically does is they'll try and record or they usually record the details of this transaction to a book that they maintain in the store.

279
00:36:49,646 --> 00:36:55,652
And then after that, they can hand the money to the user and obviously give them back their ID.

280
00:36:56,654 --> 00:37:02,048
But sometimes there's a lot of privacy issues in this process that I just highlighted here.

281
00:37:02,318 --> 00:37:08,128
So first of all, handing over your ID to an agent actually comes with its own privacy issues.

282
00:37:08,288 --> 00:37:16,128
And as this user from this study pointed out, if someone has your ID number, they could use it to do a wide variety of things.

283
00:37:16,288 --> 00:37:20,528
For example, they could register a SIM card in your name and so on and so forth.

284
00:37:20,608 --> 00:37:28,804
And so just the fact that your ID information is in fact handed to the agent can have its own privacy issues.

285
00:37:30,222 --> 00:37:31,568
But it just doesn't end there.

286
00:37:31,728 --> 00:37:38,528
Even for this message that the agent gets, it often contains a lot of your personal information, right?

287
00:37:38,830 --> 00:37:45,968
And so, first of all, your name is shared with the agent, but also your phone number is shared with the agent as well.

288
00:37:46,208 --> 00:37:55,568
And again, as you can see from this quote here from user 19 who we talked to as part of the study, the agent might try to call off stock Q.

289
00:37:55,808 --> 00:37:58,208
And it's actually it's not unheard of.

290
00:37:58,608 --> 00:38:07,248
Agents have in fact called their customers after transactions, which obviously raises a lot of privacy issues.

291
00:38:07,566 --> 00:38:17,444
And so to try and make this process better, we essentially redesigned the whole process and came up with a new model that can hopefully protect people's privacy.

292
00:38:18,254 --> 00:38:23,300
And how this new model looks like, so you have the user once again, and you have the agent.

293
00:38:23,568 --> 00:38:30,048
And now instead of the user handing over their ID to the agent, what they do is they directly authenticate with the provider.

294
00:38:30,128 --> 00:38:36,368
So this could be uh this could be through a fingerprint or a biometric when it comes to users that use a smartphone.

295
00:38:36,608 --> 00:38:41,728
But even for those that don't use a smartphone, they can essentially verify themselves using their voice.

296
00:38:41,808 --> 00:38:45,568
So SafariCon, for example, has something known as Gitambulation.

297
00:38:45,888 --> 00:38:48,848
You can just use your voice to directly authenticate with them.

298
00:38:49,134 --> 00:38:54,928
And so once this is done, the user can then proceed to initiate this transaction.

299
00:38:55,088 --> 00:38:57,700
And as you can see, this is the message they get.

300
00:38:57,870 --> 00:39:03,076
But now they also additionally get this unique code here, and I'll come back to this code in a minute.

301
00:39:03,598 --> 00:39:11,248
And then once they do this, obviously the telco is going to generate a message that is sent to the agent as well.

302
00:39:11,328 --> 00:39:17,808
And in this case, you have this message here, and it also generates a code that the agent can see.

303
00:39:17,888 --> 00:39:24,740
And it's important to know that this is essentially the same code that the user sees on their side, but also the agent sees the same code.

304
00:39:25,166 --> 00:39:27,808
And now, for authentication to actually happen.

305
00:39:27,888 --> 00:39:38,608
what the user needs to do is to just share this code with the agent, and they can verify that this is indeed the user that just did this transaction right now.

306
00:39:38,688 --> 00:39:40,928
and they don't have to hand over their ID.

307
00:39:41,008 --> 00:39:46,048
but at the same time, the user's name and phone number are now not shared with the agent anymore.

308
00:39:46,208 --> 00:39:54,660
So a lot of the privacy, a lot of a lot of the private information that was now being shared with the agent is totally removed from this process.

309
00:39:54,958 --> 00:40:04,608
And so after we essentially came up with new pro this new process, we also wanted to understand what user perceptions and challenges with this process would actually be.

310
00:40:04,910 --> 00:40:08,928
And so I once again traveled back to Kenya to carry out a study.

311
00:40:09,198 --> 00:40:27,648
We essentially created prototypes of these processes just on paper and had users, both use actually just users interact with them in a wizard of Oz type of study where they would essentially select things and move them around depending on the selection that they made.

312
00:40:27,728 --> 00:40:36,356
And then for agents, we just walked them through this new process to understand what they thought about it and if there's anything that they thought could be made better.

313
00:40:37,678 --> 00:40:41,408
So let me share some of the things we learned from doing this study.

314
00:40:41,488 --> 00:40:47,236
And in particular, I'm going to start by some of the things that both users and agents liked about this new process.

315
00:40:47,790 --> 00:40:57,008
So one of the things that users and agents really appreciated about this new process is that less personally identifiable information is now shared with the agent.

316
00:40:57,088 --> 00:41:07,008
And as this user put it, there's now no way the agent can use my details, unlike the existing one, where they can get my phone number and start calling and harassing me.

317
00:41:07,088 --> 00:41:14,212
So users really appreciate the, really appreciated the fact that their personal information is now not shared with the agent.

318
00:41:15,278 --> 00:41:38,848
A lot of users also perceive this new process to be secure, mostly based on the fact that it now uses some biometric when it comes to authentication, and they are now also directly authenticating with the mobile money provider instead of again, maybe handing over their ID to the agent, which obviously sometimes people can forge IDs and so on.

319
00:41:39,008 --> 00:41:43,236
So a lot of users also perceive this new process to be secure.

320
00:41:43,790 --> 00:41:52,288
And then lastly, one of the things that an overwhelming majority of users really appreciated is the convenience of this new process, right?

321
00:41:52,528 --> 00:41:59,728
So with the old workflow, users really have to carry their ID everywhere they go if they want to transact.

322
00:41:59,808 --> 00:42:10,980
But with this new process, essentially the ID number is removed from the equation, and this is much more convenient for users because they now can't lose their ID walking around with it and so on.

323
00:42:11,918 --> 00:42:18,528
But at the same time, there's some things that both users and agents did not like about this new process.

324
00:42:18,608 --> 00:42:21,860
And I'm just going to maybe focus on the last two.

325
00:42:22,606 --> 00:42:24,848
So the first one was around accessibility concerns.

326
00:42:24,928 --> 00:42:34,448
So we had a lot of both users and agents tell us that some people are construction workers, and so their fingerprints might not necessarily be clear.

327
00:42:34,528 --> 00:42:47,172
And so they were really worried about how this new process could potentially work, especially because it relied primarily on this biometric form of authentication.

328
00:42:48,608 --> 00:42:56,836
And then lastly, some agents actually worried when it comes to users doing the self-authentication on their side, right?

329
00:42:57,168 --> 00:43:02,528
And so a lot of them are used to actually trying to verify or authenticate the users themselves.

330
00:43:02,608 --> 00:43:13,412
And some were very worried that some users were either just going to struggle with this new process or they just didn't trust that users would be able to do this effectively.

331
00:43:14,702 --> 00:43:18,048
Anyways, what are some of the takeaways and implications from the study?

332
00:43:18,128 --> 00:43:21,728
We overall found that users and agents overall preferred our new process.

333
00:43:21,808 --> 00:43:30,244
And again, this supports our hypothesis that building privacy by default into some of these technologies does in fact have a lot of promise.

334
00:43:30,990 --> 00:43:35,488
But at the same time, we think there's a need to actually pilot and try to implement these new protocols.

335
00:43:35,648 --> 00:43:45,328
Again, just designing these things from somewhere in the US and hoping and ship and shipping and hoping they're going to work in practice.

336
00:43:45,488 --> 00:43:47,728
Back in Kenya is clearly not going to work.

337
00:43:47,888 --> 00:43:59,172
There's a lot of challenges and a lot of considerations, and there's a need to actually pilot and work with local communities to see what are some of the challenges in the process to ensure these things work in practice.

338
00:43:59,726 --> 00:44:05,408
And then just one thing that a lot of users, as I should mention, both users and agents, is around mobile money scams.

339
00:44:05,568 --> 00:44:17,700
And we are currently doing some work where we're trying to understand how scams look like in this context and trying to see whether we can improve the security and privacy of this ecosystem.

340
00:44:19,310 --> 00:44:26,960
And then lastly, just as I wrap up, I want to highlight one piece or one direction that I'm really currently excited about.

341
00:44:27,040 --> 00:44:32,132
And I'm currently collaborating on it with my colleagues here at UC Berkeley.

342
00:44:32,720 --> 00:44:44,484
And what we are essentially trying to do is to see if privacy enhancing technologies can actually allow data sharing, which would come with its own benefits, particularly in the majority world.

343
00:44:45,230 --> 00:44:48,612
Okay, just to maybe quickly give some context for this.

344
00:44:49,200 --> 00:44:54,320
So in the majority world, Kenya included, there's a lot of data that is usually generated.

345
00:44:54,510 --> 00:45:02,320
So when you think about the mobile money processes, for example, there's just a lot of data that is generated as part of this process.

346
00:45:02,480 --> 00:45:05,440
We also have a lot of health data that exists out there.

347
00:45:05,600 --> 00:45:08,720
We collect sensors every couple of years.

348
00:45:09,040 --> 00:45:13,040
there's really a lot of useful data that exists out there.

349
00:45:13,280 --> 00:45:24,932
And there's actually value in trying to share this information, whether it's with researchers or with other interested parties, this can really unlock innovation and improve our lives.

350
00:45:25,390 --> 00:45:29,920
But at the same time, it's often not possible to do this because of a lot of privacy concerns.

351
00:45:30,320 --> 00:45:38,000
So if you think about census data, for example, this there's a lot of your private information that is usually collected as part of this process.

352
00:45:38,222 --> 00:45:46,788
And you'd probably not want another person accessing that information if privacy is not uh adequately addressed.

353
00:45:47,440 --> 00:45:51,360
And this is where our proposed work actually comes in.

354
00:45:51,520 --> 00:46:01,840
And so we believe that privacy enhancing technologies can actually allow some of this data to be shared while still protecting individuals' privacy.

355
00:46:01,920 --> 00:46:09,892
But sometimes it's not really been work trying to actually deploy privacy enhancing technologies, particularly in the majority world.

356
00:46:10,240 --> 00:46:15,040
And this is the gap that we are trying to address with this uh set of work.

357
00:46:15,342 --> 00:46:25,636
So, in particular, uh what we are trying to do is we're essentially partnering with governments and NGOs in uh majority in the majority world.

358
00:46:26,000 --> 00:46:38,560
And our first goal here is to really try and understand what are some of the data sharing needs or what are some of the things that those governments or those NGOs actually want to do as it relates to data sharing.

359
00:46:38,798 --> 00:46:45,760
And this could be sharing the data with external entities, but it could also be just sharing data across ministries, for example.

360
00:46:45,920 --> 00:46:52,480
It turns out that sometimes ministries cannot even share data across themselves for a wide variety of reasons.

361
00:46:52,560 --> 00:46:58,880
So the first step in this work is just to try and understand what are some of the data sharing challenges and needs that exist.

362
00:46:59,278 --> 00:47:11,684
And then from there, we are actually trying to work backwards and either customize existing privacy enhancing technologies to tackle these challenges, also just redesign some of them and make them work in practice.

363
00:47:12,240 --> 00:47:27,556
And we are hoping that by using this workflow where we understand the needs and actually design and implement the solutions, there's a lot of useful lessons and other recommendations that we are going to be able to create out of this.

364
00:47:27,840 --> 00:47:32,240
And we are essentially going to create this privacy playbook that's going to detail.

365
00:47:32,240 --> 00:47:38,080
what are some of the challenges when it comes to deploying pets again in the majority world?

366
00:47:38,350 --> 00:47:41,040
what are some of the considerations that should be made?

367
00:47:41,646 --> 00:47:48,644
and how does this generally affect the design of security and privacy in general?

368
00:47:50,830 --> 00:47:54,080
